{"hash":"2f7ec1791f9cb3a5","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":79.761,"said":"As we cross the forecourt toward the temple steps, picture instead a prison, in a city out in the sea. Boats had come by, full of travellers, and the rakshasas had bound them all and thrown them into chains. Their leader was a merchant named Supriya. He was a devotee of Shiva so steady that, the Purana says, on any day he could not worship Shiva, he would not eat. He did not stop now. There in the prison he shaped a small linga out of earth and worshipped it, and he taught the others how. Some sat in meditation. Some worshipped Shiva in their minds. Those who knew no ritual at all simply repeated the five syllables, Namah Shivaya. And Supriya knew that every offering was being received. Six months passed this way, in chains and in prayer. Then one day one of Daruka’s guards looked in, and saw something he could not explain: a beautiful form of Shiva, standing before the merchant. The guard ran to tell his king. Climb the steps now, into the hall, and listen to what Daruka did next.\n\nDid you know? In the Shiva Purana, Supriya is a vaishya, a merchant, who wore sacred ash and rudraksha as his only ornaments.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":996},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1012,"end":1123}],"words":[[0.098,0.216,0,2],[0.326,0.108,3,5],[0.447,0.373,6,11],[0.831,0.096,12,15],[0.939,0.481,16,25],[1.432,0.325,26,32],[1.769,0.108,33,36],[1.889,0.421,37,43],[2.322,0.505,44,49],[3.055,0.361,51,58],[3.427,0.337,59,66],[3.776,0.048,67,68],[3.836,0.481,69,75],[4.557,0.12,77,79],[4.689,0.06,80,81],[4.762,0.409,82,86],[5.182,0.252,87,90],[5.447,0.084,91,93],[5.543,0.096,94,97],[5.651,0.325,98,101],[6.875,0.433,103,108],[7.32,0.216,109,112],[7.548,0.228,113,117],[7.788,0.325,118,120],[8.317,0.216,122,126],[8.546,0.132,127,129],[8.69,0.625,130,140],[9.459,0.156,142,145],[9.627,0.084,146,149],[9.724,0.661,150,159],[10.397,0.168,160,163],[10.577,0.288,164,169],[10.877,0.252